  "guidance": [
    "Only needed on the AC-inverter mini-split path; a native DC AC unit skips the inverter entirely.",
    "Must be pure sine wave (AC compressors and electronics require it).",
    "Two classes: Combined (all-in-one = inverter+MPPT+charger, simplest, higher idle, fixed PV window) vs Standalone (inverter only + a separate MPPT, more boxes, frugal idle, free MPPT choice).",
    "Bus-voltage coupling: a 2 kW load pulls ~170 A at 12 V, ~85 A at 24 V, ~42 A at 48 V. 12 V inverters cap ~2-3 kW and run hot; the best 2-3 kW and all-in-one units are 24 V or 48 V.",
    "Idle draw matters on a solar-direct daytime build (runs for hours; every 10 W idle ~= 80 Wh/day). Frugal: Victron Phoenix/MultiPlus ~8-15 W. Hungry: all-in-ones + AIMS ~25-70 W.",
    "The EG4 3000EHV is the all-in-one exception on PV: its 500 V input strings cheap 72-cell surplus panels like a good standalone MPPT would.",
    "Standalone system cost must add a separate MPPT (~$250-280) to compare apples-to-apples with an all-in-one."
  ],
